# Line endings are stored as written.  Git converts nothing, on any platform.
#
# THE BREAKAGE THIS PREVENTS.  This tree is deliberately mixed: 333 of its
# Python files are stored LF and 30 text files are stored CRLF, the four Noah
# tables among them, and the carried device sources are LF because the tree
# they were carved from stores them that way.  Under git's default handling a
# clone made with core.autocrlf set rewrites all of those on checkout, and the
# next tool that reads and rewrites a file commits the rewrite.  It happened
# here: one commit converted a test file whole and nine other test files went
# the other way in the same round, 7,639 added and 7,591 deleted lines of diff
# carrying 428 real changed lines, in the branch whose whole change was test
# assertions.  Nobody can review 428 lines inside 15,230.
#
# `-text` is the pin: what a writer wrote is what is stored and what comes
# back out.  It does not stop a tool from rewriting a file's endings, which is
# what `tools/check_line_endings.py` measures and `tests/test_line_endings.py`
# holds.
* -text
